1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This is School Plan Note, a web application
for elementary school teachers in Japan.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,080
Teachers use it to write weekly lesson plans,
manage classroom tasks, and share class

3
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:17,000
newsletters.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,775
Our homepage is served from
schoolplan-editor.giga-school.com, a domain we

5
00:00:21,775 --> 00:00:25,000
own and have verified in Search Console.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,257
The privacy policy is on that same domain —
this is the URL submitted in this verification

7
00:00:31,257 --> 00:00:33,000
request.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
And these are our terms of service.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Each teacher owns a single Google Spreadsheet
that stores their own lesson plan data.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,949
The application creates this spreadsheet
itself the first time a teacher signs in. That

11
00:00:53,949 --> 00:00:58,000
is why the drive.file scope alone is enough to
read and write it.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,828
It never opens any other spreadsheet in the
teacher’s Drive, and it never sees another

13
00:01:03,828 --> 00:01:06,000
teacher’s data.

14
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Opening the web application starts the OAuth
flow.

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
This is the OAuth consent screen for our
application.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
The address bar shows the client ID of the
OAuth client submitted for verification.

17
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,889
The application requests seven scopes:
per-file Drive access, sending email as the

18
00:01:46,889 --> 00:01:50,961
user, creating scheduled triggers, outbound
HTTPS requests, Classroom courses read-only,

19
00:01:50,961 --> 00:01:54,000
Classroom announcements, and the user’s email
address.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Every one of them is demonstrated end to end
in the following sections of this video.

21
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Teachers enter their weekly lesson plan in
this grid.

22
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,358
What we type here is written into the
teacher’s own spreadsheet through the Sheets

23
00:02:32,358 --> 00:02:34,000
API.

24
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,685
The same file is read back through the same
scope: this annual lesson-hour summary is

25
00:02:41,685 --> 00:02:45,000
calculated from the rows stored in it.

26
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,062
Creating and deleting a task writes to, and
removes rows from, the same spreadsheet.

27
00:03:09,062 --> 00:03:13,315
Deleted rows are moved to a recycle-bin sheet
inside that same file, so nothing is lost by

28
00:03:13,315 --> 00:03:15,000
accident.

29
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,845
Here is the teacher’s spreadsheet in their own
Google account, showing exactly the changes

30
00:03:22,845 --> 00:03:26,000
made in the app a moment ago.

31
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,541
We do not hold the spreadsheets scope. This
file is read and written through drive.file,

32
00:03:47,541 --> 00:03:52,000
because the app created it. We cannot open any
other spreadsheet.

33
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,309
This panel shows which account is signed in
and which single file is being used. We

34
00:04:02,309 --> 00:04:06,899
request the user’s email address only to tell
one teacher’s database from another’s — it is

35
00:04:06,899 --> 00:04:09,000
never sent anywhere.

36
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,595
A teacher who already has a lesson plan file
can point the app at it. This is the only way

37
00:04:18,595 --> 00:04:22,000
the app can reach a file it did not create.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,949
This is the Google Picker. The teacher chooses
one file, and only that file becomes visible

39
00:04:42,949 --> 00:04:47,859
to the application. Everything else in their
Drive stays out of reach — the app has no way

40
00:04:47,859 --> 00:04:50,000
to list or open it.

41
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,028
The school calendar feature works the same
way. Teachers pick the PDF files they want the

42
00:04:59,028 --> 00:05:01,000
app to use.

43
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,523
The picker is filtered to PDF files and allows
multiple selection. The app stores only a

44
00:05:23,523 --> 00:05:29,000
reference to the files the teacher selected,
and can read those files and nothing else.

45
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,760
The newsletter editor stores each saved
newsletter as a file the app creates in the

46
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,000
teacher’s Drive.

47
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Deleting it from the app moves that Drive file
to the trash of the teacher’s own account.

48
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,115
A backup copies the whole database into a new
spreadsheet, again created by the app. Backups

49
00:06:36,115 --> 00:06:41,000
older than the retention limit are moved to
the trash automatically.

50
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:48,298
Here is the teacher’s Drive. These are the
files the application created: the lesson plan

51
00:06:48,298 --> 00:06:53,000
database, the newsletter files, and the backup
we just made.

52
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,253
And here is the Drive trash, showing the
newsletter file we deleted a moment ago. Every

53
00:07:02,253 --> 00:07:07,000
write and every delete is visible in the
user’s own account.

54
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:16,129
We request drive.file, not full Drive access.
The app can only see files it created itself

55
00:07:16,129 --> 00:07:19,000
or files the teacher picked.

56
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:30,308
The app calls the Gemini API to draft the
class newsletter from the teacher’s lesson

57
00:07:30,308 --> 00:07:32,000
plan.

58
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:40,000
This outbound HTTPS request is what the
script.external_request scope is for.

59
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,682
The same scope powers the other AI features:
extracting tasks and school events from a PDF,

60
00:08:03,682 --> 00:08:08,000
drafting the weekly reflection, and laying out
teaching units.

61
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:17,222
This model list is itself an outbound request:
the app asks the Gemini API which models the

62
00:08:17,222 --> 00:08:20,000
teacher’s own key can use.

63
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,003
The API key is supplied and stored by each
teacher in their own Apps Script user

64
00:08:25,003 --> 00:08:29,071
properties. We never receive it, and we
require a paid-tier key, because free-tier

65
00:08:29,071 --> 00:08:32,000
content may be used to improve Google’s
models.

66
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,531
This is every host the app contacts: the
Gemini API, the Google Drive and Google Sheets

67
00:08:35,531 --> 00:08:39,012
REST APIs, Google’s tokeninfo endpoint to
identify our own Cloud project for the file

68
00:08:39,012 --> 00:08:42,420
picker, and a public holiday calendar
published by the Japanese Cabinet Office. No

69
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:46,000
user data is sent to that last one, and there
is no server of ours anywhere in this list.

70
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Teachers can enable a daily reminder for their
unfinished tasks.

71
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:07,216
Setting the delivery time creates a time-based
trigger, which requires the script.scriptapp

72
00:09:07,216 --> 00:09:09,000
scope.

73
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:20,696
The script.send_mail scope sends that
reminder, and userinfo.email identifies the

74
00:09:20,696 --> 00:09:27,000
signed-in teacher so the message goes to that
same teacher and no one else.

75
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,805
Here is the message in the signed-in teacher’s
own Gmail inbox. The To field is that same

76
00:09:49,805 --> 00:09:54,610
teacher. This reminder is the only email the
app can send: there is no recipient list and

77
00:09:54,610 --> 00:09:58,000
no other template, and the app cannot read any
mail.

78
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:08,000
The teacher links the app to their own Google
Classroom course.

79
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,783
We use classroom.courses.readonly only to list
the active courses the teacher already

80
00:10:17,783 --> 00:10:22,366
teaches, so they can choose one from this
dropdown. We do not read student data,

81
00:10:22,366 --> 00:10:25,000
rosters, coursework, or grades.

82
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:45,000
The teacher can post tomorrow’s schedule to
the selected course as an announcement.

83
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:00,770
The second kind of post attaches a file: the
newsletter is exported as a PDF that the app

84
00:11:00,770 --> 00:11:06,000
creates in the teacher’s Drive, and that file
is attached to the announcement.

85
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,571
A teacher who wants this every day can set a
posting time here. That schedule is a trigger

86
00:11:20,571 --> 00:11:25,212
in their own account, and it posts only to the
course they selected. Leaving the field empty

87
00:11:25,212 --> 00:11:27,000
removes it.

88
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,674
Here are both announcements in Google
Classroom, in the teacher’s own course. The

89
00:11:52,674 --> 00:11:57,509
app posts only to the course the teacher
chose, and only when the teacher asks for it

90
00:11:57,509 --> 00:12:00,000
or schedules it themselves.

91
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:11,038
The script.scriptapp scope schedules work in
the teacher’s own account. This panel lists

92
00:12:11,038 --> 00:12:15,000
every trigger the app has created there.

93
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,779
The daily reminder and the Classroom posting
time we just set now appear here, next to the

94
00:12:22,779 --> 00:12:27,408
nightly job that cleans up triggers which are
no longer needed. Importing a PDF adds a

95
00:12:27,408 --> 00:12:32,000
short-lived trigger to this list too, and it
removes itself when the import finishes.

96
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,716
Deleting it here removes the trigger from the
teacher’s account immediately, as the list

97
00:12:51,716 --> 00:12:56,357
confirms. Teachers can see and remove every
scheduled job the app created, and the app

98
00:12:56,357 --> 00:12:59,000
creates none that are not listed.

99
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,543
To summarize: School Plan Note keeps each
teacher’s lesson plan in one spreadsheet it

100
00:13:05,543 --> 00:13:09,161
created, creates and deletes only its own
files in Drive or the ones the teacher picked,

101
00:13:09,161 --> 00:13:12,704
sends a reminder to the signed-in teacher
alone, schedules that work in the teacher’s

102
00:13:12,704 --> 00:13:16,247
own account where they can remove it, and
posts to the teacher’s own Classroom course

103
00:13:16,247 --> 00:13:18,000
when they ask.

104
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:26,000
All requested scopes are used only for these
purposes, as described in our privacy policy.

105
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Thank you for reviewing our application.
